WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.468
Endpoint and system telemetry provides activity that network observation may not reveal.

00:00:05.468 --> 00:00:09.046
Engineering workstations, HMIs, application servers, historians, jump hosts,

00:00:09.046 --> 00:00:13.520
and remote-access systems can record users, processes, files, services, configurations,

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:14.862
and local events.

00:00:14.862 --> 00:00:19.335
Authentication records show successful and failed logons, account use, privilege,

00:00:19.335 --> 00:00:20.677
and session timing.

00:00:20.677 --> 00:00:25.150
File telemetry may reveal project access, new executables, configuration changes,

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:26.492
or removable-media activity.

00:00:26.492 --> 00:00:29.623
Service events can identify startup, shutdown, failure,

00:00:29.623 --> 00:00:33.202
or changes to applications that support control visibility.

00:00:33.202 --> 00:00:38.122
Engineering workstations are especially important because specialized tools can configure controllers,

00:00:38.122 --> 00:00:40.359
HMIs, networks, and field devices.

00:00:40.359 --> 00:00:45.279
Relevant records may include project versions, downloads, connection targets, firmware work,

00:00:45.279 --> 00:00:46.621
and change approvals.

00:00:46.621 --> 00:00:50.200
These actions need strong identity and maintenance context.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:55.568
HMI, historian, and SCADA servers can provide alarms, application errors, tag changes,

00:00:55.568 --> 00:00:57.804
database activity, and collection health.

00:00:57.804 --> 00:01:01.383
Remote-access gateways add source, destination, approval, session duration,

00:01:01.383 --> 00:01:07.198
and sometimes activity records that connect an external identity to an internal asset.

00:01:07.198 --> 00:01:10.776
Collection must be compatible with the supported system.

00:01:10.776 --> 00:01:13.013
An endpoint agent, audit setting,

00:01:13.013 --> 00:01:18.828
or aggressive log forwarding configuration can consume resources or disrupt a legacy application.

00:01:18.828 --> 00:01:24.643
Test changes with engineering and vendors, monitor overhead, and define a rollback path.

00:01:24.643 --> 00:01:29.564
Normalize endpoint records with asset role, site, owner, and reliable time.

00:01:29.564 --> 00:01:34.484
Protect logs from unauthorized change and retain enough history for investigation.

00:01:34.484 --> 00:01:39.405
Cross-source correlation is strongest: a user logon, remote session, engineering connection,

00:01:39.405 --> 00:01:45.220
controller change, and process event together provide more confidence than any one record.
